  "notes": "Rap-to-country crossover — a rasped confessional baritone over gospel-rock and trap-rock beds, windowed to the 2021–2025 country pivot only, which the `memphis-rap` researcher explicitly left unclaimed. The key concentration is documented: \"C major is his home key to a remarkable degree (a large block of album cuts read C), then G/E♭/A♭/B, with minor cuts in B minor/D minor/C minor,\" and the tempo band has \"a very distinctive ~83–87 cluster across a large share of his catalogue,\" so the mode sits at 85 in a 70–110 window. The entry names the primary dynamic device and this model is built around it: \"programmed clap/snap on the backbeat with 16th hats and sampled sub-kicks in verses, opening to a full live rock kit with a big reverbed snare on choruses; half-time bridges and hard drop-outs. Model the verse→chorus kit swap as the primary dynamic device\" — hence the widest velocity-tier spread in this batch and a high `fullTimeVariantProb`. The 808 is `counter_riff` because the entry gives both layers a stage: \"tuned sub carrying verses, joined or replaced by a distorted electric bass at the chorus.\" Harmony is \"I–V–vi–IV and vi–IV–I–V with gospel IV–iv–I borrows; i–VI–III–VII on the minor cuts.\" Melody is \"rasped baritone, conversational verse density (rap-derived phrasing, 4–6 syllables per beat), chorus on long sustained notes with a cracked peak.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register, the full-time variant share and the counter ratio.",
